{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgj7gHwGEr3N2iqCnBHPm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01T7tyqb5CDjvS16quMVedJL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat paper3/latex/sec3_beyond.tex && echo ====== && sed -n 1,80p paper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex","description":"Read Section 3 and start of Section 2"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3816,"cache_read_input_tokens":86258,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3816,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"670fc671-9721-48eb-82d9-ea6bc5bd38c4","uuid":"bec0d545-dc3e-4b0d-85c8-fd667dcf2540","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:02:26.385Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj7gHbBrHXMQpYzNREYQ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01T7tyqb5CDjvS16quMVedJL":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && cat paper3/latex/sec3_beyond.tex && echo ====== && sed -n 1,80p paper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex","description":"Read Section 3 and start of Section 2"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01T7tyqb5CDjvS16quMVedJL":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T7tyqb5CDjvS16quMVedJL","type":"tool_result","content":"% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015\n% item 03 (2026-11-05). Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and\n% the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the\n% line + 1); every Lean location is in the clone at commit 4eaee875, read with\n% cat/sed -n only. Every page, line, fragment and number of this section is\n% checked and the two tables are written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved.\n\\section{What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond}\n\nThe manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nis 67 pages; the comparator statement is 220 lines. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by one\nand says, for each, whether it is what the Lean theorem asserts, whether it\nfollows from the Lean theorem by an argument that exists only on paper, or\nwhether it rests on a bound that the Lean library does not contain. The\ncollection's own scope note draws the line in two sentences, which we quote\nin full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines 9 and 11):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\\sloppy\n``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly\ncounts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$\nvariables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,\nwhere $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has\nvisited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''\n\n``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for\nfixed $D > 0$, this improves to\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''\n\\end{quote}\nEverything below is an elaboration of that note against the manuscript's\ntext and the library's files. Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.\n\n\\subsection{Three statuses, and what ``paper-only'' means}\n\\label{sec:beyond-statuses}\n\nWe tag every claim with one of three words.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Verified.} The claim is the comparator statement\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, or one of its conjuncts, or a theorem of\n  the solution tree that the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} compiled\n  and whose axioms it printed. Nothing else is called verified.\n\\item \\textbf{Supported.} The claim follows from the verified statement by\n  an argument that is on paper only: a reduction of a few lines, or a\n  citation. The argument is not in the Lean library, and we have not\n  checked it; what we have checked is that the only property of the\n  counter it uses is one the verified statement provides, and that the\n  exponent it inherits is therefore $\\rhon$, not a sharper one.\n\\item \\textbf{Paper-only.} The claim rests on a bound that the Lean\n  library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} does not contain: Theorem~1.1's two\n  sharper exponents, and whatever is derived from them.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe brief for this section had two tags, verified and paper-only; the\nmiddle one is ours, because the manuscript's corollaries are of two\ndifferent kinds and a single word would have put Corollary~1.3 (a\ncounting-preserving encoding into the verified counter) beside\nCorollary~1.4 (a bound whose proof calls the fixed-density exponent by\nname). In the brief's partition, ``supported'' is a sub-case of\n``paper-only'': not in the library. In no case does ``paper-only'' mean\n``wrong''. We did not read the manuscript's proofs for correctness; the\ntag records where a claim is established, not whether.\n\n\\subsection{The sharper exponents of Theorem~1.1}\n\\label{sec:beyond-exponents}\n\nTheorem~1.1 (p.~2, lines 56--62) states two running-time bounds for the\nsame deterministic algorithm: $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$\nfor every $k$-CNF on $n \\ge 20$ declared variables, and\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$ when $m \\le Dn$ for a\nfixed $D > 0$; both with $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space (line 60). The\nsentence that follows the theorem is the one the title of this paper is\nabout (p.~2, lines 63--67):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker\nexponent $O_k(\\rho(n))$, where\n$\\rho(n) = n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n} = n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}$.\nAppendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.''\n\\end{quote}\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476) is the weaker bound as a\nstatement of its own: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$, the interpolation\nalgorithm counts in $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ bit operations and\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space, for $n \\ge 20$.\n\n\\paragraph{Which of the three is in the Lean.}\nThe comparator's \\runtimeScale{} (lines 132--134 of the comparator file)\nis $n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the squared form. The solution's\nown base-2 exponent \\texttt{rho}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is\n$n(\\log_2\\log_2 n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log_2 n}$, Proposition~6.4's $\\rho$ with the\nbase made explicit, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_le\\_four\\_rho} (lines\n190--191) bounds the comparator's exponent by four times it for\n$n \\ge 20$. The solution's sparsification parameters are Proposition~6.4's\nto the letter: the proof selects $\\lambda_n = \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$,\n$\\nu_n = \\lceil\\log_2\\lambda_n\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\lfloor\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}/\\nu_n^2\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\nu_n^4 \\le \\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486),\nand \\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define \\texttt{sparsifyLambda},\n\\texttt{sparsifyNu} and \\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three\nquantities in natural-number arithmetic. So the verified exponent is the\nweaker one, and the verified proof is organised around Section~6.4's\nparameters; what Appendix~D proves is not in the library in any form we\ncould find (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}). The general exponent\n$n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ and the fixed-density exponent\n$n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ are \\textbf{paper-only}: proved at p.~57 (lines\n3222--3246) and p.~54 (lines 3045--3064) respectively, by the contraction\ntheorem of Appendix~D.\n\n\\paragraph{Both bounds from one algorithm.}\nThe manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both paper-only\nbounds, switching on $m \\le n\\nu$ (p.~57, lines 3247--3250). That\nalgorithm is not the verified one either.\n\n\\paragraph{The three exponents, in numbers.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} puts the three exponents side by side,\neach divided by $n$ and each at constant $1$. The verified ratio is the\none of Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. The general paper-only ratio,\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$, never reaches $1$: it peaks at $2/e = 0.736$\nwhere $\\ln n = e^2$, that is $n = e^{e^2} \\approx 1{,}618$, and falls on\nboth sides. The fixed-density ratio $1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ decreases from\n$0.578$ at $n = 20$. So, at constant $1$, both paper-only guarantees sit\nbelow $2^n$ at every size, and the verified one sits above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ to $n \\approx 10^{2390}$. That is the whole content of the\ntitle's qualifier ``verified'': the manuscript's exponents would make a\ndifferent sentence, and they are not what the kernel checked. Two\ncautions apply to the table equally. The constants in front of every\nexponent are $O_k(1)$ or $O_{k,D}(1)$ and are not computed anywhere, in\nthe manuscript or in the Lean; and the manuscript's exponents are\nundefined below $n = e$ and are stated for $n \\ge 20$, while the\nverified one is stated for all $n$ (the $+16$). Figure~\\ref{fig:central}\ndraws the same three curves.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The three exponents of the manuscript divided by $n$, at constant\n$1$: the verified $\\rhon/n = (\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$\n(comparator lines 132--134); Theorem~1.1's general exponent\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 59); its fixed-density exponent\n$1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 62). Natural logarithms throughout (p.~1,\nlines 48--49). The row $e^{e^2}$ is the peak of the general ratio\n($2/e$). Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-exponents}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/beyond_exponents}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{The abstract's form.}\nThe abstract states the running time as\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}}$ (p.~1, lines 6--9). Since\n$(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = (\\ln n)^{o(1)}$, the verified exponent is of that form\ntoo, and the manuscript says so at lines 64--66. We tag the abstract's\nform \\textbf{verified}, with the remark that its $o(1)$ absorbs exactly\nthe factor the three rows of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} differ by:\nat $n = 10^6$ it is $(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = 6.9$ for the verified exponent,\n$\\ln\\ln n = 2.6$ for the general one and $1$ for the fixed-density one.\n\n\\subsection{The corollaries}\n\\label{sec:beyond-corollaries}\n\n\\paragraph{Finding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p.~2, lines 68--71).}\n``When $\\#F > 0$, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying\nassignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the\nremaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\nconditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying\nassignment,'' with polynomial expected overhead and a citation.\n\\textbf{Supported}: the self-reduction calls the verified counter at\nmost $n$ times on formulas of the same width, and the verified statement\nprovides the exact count it needs. The reduction is two sentences of\nprose and is not in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.2, Max-$k$-SAT within $\\varepsilon m$ (p.~2, lines 77--94).}\nA randomised algorithm finds an assignment satisfying at least\n$\\mathrm{OPT}(F) - \\varepsilon m$ clauses with probability\n$1 - 2^{-\\Omega(n)}$ in time\n\\[\n  (n+m)^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(\\rho(n))}\n\\]\n(line 80). The corollary is stated with $\\rho(n)$ itself, the\nverified exponent. Its proof samples $O(n/\\varepsilon^2)$ clauses,\nbuilds a threshold circuit of size $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$, reduces it to\na 3-CNF with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables by a cited lemma, and calls\nTheorem~1.1 with binary search and self-reduction (lines 82--94).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the exponent matches the verified one because\n$\\rho(cn) = O_c(\\rho(n))$, and every call is to a fixed-width counter\non $O(n)$ variables; the sampling, the circuit and the reduction are on\npaper. Its randomness is in the reduction, not in the counter.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.3, independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings (p.~2--3, lines 100--120).}\nThe number of independent sets and, for fixed $q$, of proper\n$q$-colourings of a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges in $2^{o(v)}$\ntime, and the number of perfect matchings in $2^{o(v+e)}$ time, all in\npolynomial space. The encodings are explicit: one variable per vertex\nand a 2-clause per edge (lines 107--112); $q$ variables per vertex with\nexactly-one and no-equal-colour clauses, a $q$-CNF on $qv$ variables\n(lines 112--115); one variable per edge and a linear-size circuit for\n``exactly one selected edge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with\n$O(v+e)$ variables, whose gate variables are determined by the edge\nbits so that the count is preserved (lines 116--119).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the conclusion is $2^{o(\\cdot)}$, which needs only\n$\\rhon = o(n)$ \\leanref{rho-little-o}, and each encoding is\ncounting-preserving into a fixed-width CNF on $O(v)$ or $O(v+e)$\nvariables. None of the three encodings is in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.4, a circuit lower bound for $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ (p.~3, lines 121--127).}\n``The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for\n$\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$'' (line 121): a language $L \\in \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\nand a constant $c > 0$ with $C_L(n) > c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ for infinitely\nmany $n$, where $C_L(n)$ counts non-input fan-in-two gates (lines\n123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967),\nWilliams's witness-circuit argument with a local reduction, and it\ninvokes the paper-only exponent in so many words: ``Put\n$a(t) = \\sqrt{\\log_2 t}$. Testing whether the count in the\nconstant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is positive decides 3-SAT with $V$\nvariables and at most $5V$ clauses in time $V^b\\,2^{AV/a(V)}$ for fixed\n$A, b > 0$'' (p.~52, lines 2949--2950); the threshold\n$s_n = \\lfloor\\eta n\\lfloor a(n)\\rfloor\\rfloor$ is chosen against that\n$a$ (line 2953), and the constant $c$ comes out as\n$\\sqrt{\\eta/(d\\log 2)}$ (line 2967). \\textbf{Paper-only}: the\nquantitative bound $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ is tied to $2^{AV/\\sqrt{\\log V}}$,\nwhich the library does not contain. What the same argument would give\nfrom the verified exponent is a question about the manuscript's proof,\nwhich we do not examine (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-not}).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}} \\not\\subseteq \\mathrm{SIZE}(O(n))$ (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}\n``Williams proved that if ETH is false, then $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\ndoes not have linear-size circuits [Wil13, Theorem 6.1]. Apply this\nimplication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\nsupplied by Theorem 1.1.'' \\textbf{Supported}: the only input is a\ndeterministic $2^{o(n)}$ 3-SAT decision, which the verified statement\nprovides on its answer tape for $k = 3$; the implication is a citation.\nThe manuscript's own scope sentence applies: the corollary ``does not\nassert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP'' (lines 2923--2924).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.2, inverting constant-locality maps (p.~52, lines 2974--2977).}\nFor a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits,\nthe preimage count $|G^{-1}(\\vec b)|$ and a preimage in deterministic\ntime $(n+m)^{O_d(1)}\\,2^{O_d(\\rho(n))}$ and polynomial space, for\n$n \\ge 20$. Stated with $\\rho(n)$. The proof writes one clause per\nforbidden local assignment, a CNF of width at most $d$ on the original\n$n$ variables with at most $2^d m$ clauses whose satisfying assignments\nare exactly the preimages, and applies Theorem~1.1 with\n$k = \\max\\{2, d\\}$ (lines 2979--2988). \\textbf{Supported}: the\nencoding is on paper; the counter and the exponent are the verified\nones. The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way\nfunctions (p.~53, lines 2994--2999), inherits the same status and the\nmanuscript's own limit, ``This does not rule out one-wayness against\npolynomial-time algorithms'' (line 2999).\n\n\\subsection{Appendix D: the contraction route}\n\\label{sec:beyond-appendix-d}\n\nAppendix~D, ``A sharper bound for exact counting'' (p.~53--64, from line\n3001), proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The\ntwo running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines\n3002--3003). Theorem~D.1 (p.~53, lines 3017--3028) is the contraction\ntheorem: a bipartite tensor network with at most $Cs$ edges, maximum\ndegree at most $\\lceil s^{9/10}\\rceil$ and entries in\n$(\\mathbb{Z}/2^{P_0}\\mathbb{Z})[V]/(V^\\ell)$ is contracted in\n$(s+L)^{O(1)}\\,2^{O(s/\\sqrt{\\log s})}$ bit operations and polynomial\nspace. The fixed-density proof (p.~54, lines 3045--3064) encodes a\nformula as a network with $O_k(n+m)$ edges and applies Theorem~D.1 with\n$s = n + m$; the arbitrary-density proof (p.~57, lines 3222--3246)\ncombines the sparsification of Section~6 with Lemma~D.2 (p.~54, line\n3069) and reaches the exponent $O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})$ at line\n3243. Section~D.4 changes the logarithm convention for its own proof,\n``log denotes a base-two logarithm and ln denotes a natural logarithm''\n(p.~57, lines 3251--3252). All of this is \\textbf{paper-only}: no\ndeclaration about tensor networks, contraction, Hermite recovery or\npackets exists in the family's 3,977 files, by the identifier search of\nSection~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}. We make no statement about Appendix~D's\nargument; we did not read it for correctness.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses of \\S1.1.1 and the fine-grained remarks of \\S1.1.3}\n\\label{sec:beyond-hypotheses}\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.1 (p.~4, lines 187--191).}\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [\\ldots] and SETH [\\ldots], and of Gap-ETH.''\nAnd \\S1.1.2 (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves\n\\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time and\ntherefore refutes \\#ETH.'' \\textbf{Supported}, and by the shortest\nargument in the manuscript: the verified statement is a deterministic\nmachine whose answer tape carries $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ and whose\ncount tape carries the count, within $2^{o(n)}$ times a polynomial, for\neach fixed $k \\ge 2$; each hypothesis is the negation of that. The\nhypotheses themselves are not defined in the library (the strings\n``ETH'' and ``SETH'' occur in the collection's Lean only in two files of\na different family, \\clonefile{lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/},\nwhich take an ETH-like proposition as a hypothesis and are not in the\nclosure). Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} states each hypothesis with its\nquantifiers and says exactly how the verified statement negates it; the\ntwo sentences the manuscript adds at lines 191--194, on unconditional\nlower bounds for restricted proof systems and on Williams's 2016\nMA-SETH protocol, are context, not claims about the result.\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.3 (p.~5, lines 213--243).}\nAfter a list of lower bounds proved from ETH or SETH (lines 213--225),\nthe manuscript writes: ``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis\nfor the hardness claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from\nweaker hypotheses that may still hold'' (lines 226--227), names the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture (lines 229--232) and the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$\nalgorithm, adding: ``Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each\nfixed clause width $k$, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow\nwith $n$'' (lines 235--236). These are \\textbf{paper-only} remarks in\nthe sense that nothing about any other hypothesis or any reduction is in\nthe library; they are also the manuscript's own statement of what the\nresult does not do, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} rests on them.\nThe one positive transfer, Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$\nby one variable per element and two clauses per set (lines 237--243), is\n\\textbf{supported} in the same sense as Corollary~1.3: an encoding on\npaper into the verified counter, inheriting $\\rhon$. The caveat at\np.~2, lines 95--99, that $2^{o(n)}$ bounds ``do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions,'' is not a claim but a limit the\nmanuscript places on its own result; it is quoted again in\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences}.\n\n\\subsection{What the library carries beyond the comparator statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond-lean}\n\n``Paper-only'' is defined against the whole library at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}, not only against the comparator file, so we say what\nelse the library proves about this family. \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}\n(201 lines) states five theorems, each followed by a \\texttt{\\#print\naxioms} directive (lines 197--201), and the replay's build log carries\nthe kernel's answer for all five: the three standard axioms\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms\\_in\\_build.txt}).\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 10) and\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 74): the comparator statement,\n  first over the explicit 80-register, 1,220-tape program and then over\n  the machine it compiles to (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} (lines 104--147): the same conclusion\n  with the polynomial factor written as $(n + |F| + 1)^{d}$ instead of\n  $(L+1)^d$, the exponent as $A\\cdot\\mathtt{rho}\\ n$ in base 2, and the\n  proviso $20 \\le n$ (line 109); its proof takes $2d$, $2e$ and $4A$ from\n  the comparator theorem's constants (line 132). This is Proposition~6.4's\n  $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ form in the library, so the\n  manuscript's polynomial factor is verified as well as its exponent; it\n  is \\textbf{verified}, and it is still the weaker exponent.\n\\item \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} (lines 149--183), for $k \\ge 3$:\n  the answer bit satisfies \\texttt{answer = true $\\leftrightarrow$\n  SAT.Satisfiable F}, the decision form made explicit.\n\\item \\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main} (line 184): the proposition\n  \\texttt{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean}, line 37), a decision-only\n  target for every width $k \\ge 3$ with the same \\texttt{runtimeBound},\n  stated there as a \\texttt{Prop} ``no inhabitant is supplied here'' and\n  inhabited in \\path{Final.lean}.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\begin{sloppypar}\nBeyond these, an identifier search over the family's 3,977 \\texttt{.lean}\nfiles, by the read-only script \\repofile{paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py},\nfinds no occurrence of \\texttt{SimpleGraph}, \\texttt{PerfectMatching},\n\\texttt{Coloring}, \\texttt{IndependentSet}, \\texttt{CircuitSize},\n\\texttt{OrthogonalVectors}, \\texttt{MaxSat}, \\texttt{TensorNetwork},\n\\texttt{HermiteInterp}, \\texttt{ENP}, \\texttt{SIZE},\n\\texttt{SetSplitting}, \\texttt{GapETH} or \\texttt{NSETH} as an\nidentifier: none of the corollaries, none of Appendix~D, and none of\nthe hypotheses is formalised. The comparator's JSON,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}, names one\ntheorem, \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, and no definition. A grep is\nnecessary and not sufficient (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); here it is used\nfor absence, where a false negative would require a corollary to be\nformalised under none of the words its own statement uses.\n\\end{sloppypar}\n\n\\subsection{The table}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{Every claim of the manuscript examined in this section: where it\nis stated (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of the committed extract),\nits status in the sense of Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-statuses}, and the\none-line reason. Generated, with every page and fragment checked against\nthe extract and the clone, by \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-claims}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/beyond_claims}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:beyond-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the paper-only proofs.} Appendix~D,\n  Appendix~C.1 and the two sharper bounds of Theorem~1.1 were located\n  and quoted, not read for correctness. ``Paper-only'' means not in the\n  Lean library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; it does not mean wrong, and\n  nothing here suggests it.\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the supported arguments either.} The\n  reductions behind Corollaries~1.2, 1.3, C.1, C.2 and the Set Splitting\n  transfer are standard in kind and short, and we checked only which\n  property of the counter they use. Whether each is correct as written\n  is the manuscript's, not ours.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the verified exponent would yield\n  through Corollary~1.4's argument.} A reader can rerun Appendix~C.1\n  with $a(t)$ replaced by the verified rate; we did not, because it is\n  a question about a paper-only proof.\n\\item \\textbf{No constants.} Every $O_k(1)$ and $O_{k,D}(1)$ in this\n  section is as unknown as the comparator's $C, A, S, d, e$; the\n  comparisons of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} are at constant $1$\n  and say nothing about any machine's running time.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the collection.} Whether the sharper bounds\n  have since been formalised elsewhere, or the manuscript revised, is\n  not examined; what is read is the commit and the dated manuscript.\n\\end{itemize}\n======\n% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05).\n% Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone\n% (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote\n% gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n% Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).\n\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n\\label{sec:statement}\n\nThe whole of what is machine-checked about family 378 is one theorem,\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, stated in the 220-line comparator file\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} of the\ncollection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nat commit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The file carries the statement with a\n\\texttt{sorry} for its proof (line 218); the solution tree proves the same\nstatement, and the collection's comparator checks that the two agree\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). The replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\nrebuilt that proof and found the three standard axioms and nothing else.\nThis section reads the statement, definition by definition, and says what\nit asserts and what it does not. Line numbers are those of the comparator\nfile; we quote nothing from memory.\n\n\\subsection{The definitions, one by one}\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a\n\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a\n\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function\n\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean\n``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some\nliteral is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''\n(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most\n\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,\n\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments\n(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the\nnumber of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when\n\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the\nmeaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is\nallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\nallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\nmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\nliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\nends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\nelement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\nA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\nat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\nnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\nlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\nTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\nso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\nis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\nso\n\\[\n  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n\\]\nfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\ndefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\na formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\nan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\neach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\ndomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\nanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"670fc671-9721-48eb-82d9-ea6bc5bd38c4","uuid":"3392f305-5f6d-46f6-aa40-9fcdd89f481a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:02:26.401Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015\n% item 03 (2026-11-05). Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and\n% the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the\n% line + 1); every Lean location is in the clone at commit 4eaee875, read with\n% cat/sed -n only. Every page, line, fragment and number of this section is\n% checked and the two tables are written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved.\n\\section{What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond}\n\nThe manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nis 67 pages; the comparator statement is 220 lines. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by one\nand says, for each, whether it is what the Lean theorem asserts, whether it\nfollows from the Lean theorem by an argument that exists only on paper, or\nwhether it rests on a bound that the Lean library does not contain. The\ncollection's own scope note draws the line in two sentences, which we quote\nin full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines 9 and 11):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\\sloppy\n``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly\ncounts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$\nvariables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,\nwhere $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has\nvisited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''\n\n``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for\nfixed $D > 0$, this improves to\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''\n\\end{quote}\nEverything below is an elaboration of that note against the manuscript's\ntext and the library's files. Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.\n\n\\subsection{Three statuses, and what ``paper-only'' means}\n\\label{sec:beyond-statuses}\n\nWe tag every claim with one of three words.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Verified.} The claim is the comparator statement\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, or one of its conjuncts, or a theorem of\n  the solution tree that the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} compiled\n  and whose axioms it printed. Nothing else is called verified.\n\\item \\textbf{Supported.} The claim follows from the verified statement by\n  an argument that is on paper only: a reduction of a few lines, or a\n  citation. The argument is not in the Lean library, and we have not\n  checked it; what we have checked is that the only property of the\n  counter it uses is one the verified statement provides, and that the\n  exponent it inherits is therefore $\\rhon$, not a sharper one.\n\\item \\textbf{Paper-only.} The claim rests on a bound that the Lean\n  library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} does not contain: Theorem~1.1's two\n  sharper exponents, and whatever is derived from them.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe brief for this section had two tags, verified and paper-only; the\nmiddle one is ours, because the manuscript's corollaries are of two\ndifferent kinds and a single word would have put Corollary~1.3 (a\ncounting-preserving encoding into the verified counter) beside\nCorollary~1.4 (a bound whose proof calls the fixed-density exponent by\nname). In the brief's partition, ``supported'' is a sub-case of\n``paper-only'': not in the library. In no case does ``paper-only'' mean\n``wrong''. We did not read the manuscript's proofs for correctness; the\ntag records where a claim is established, not whether.\n\n\\subsection{The sharper exponents of Theorem~1.1}\n\\label{sec:beyond-exponents}\n\nTheorem~1.1 (p.~2, lines 56--62) states two running-time bounds for the\nsame deterministic algorithm: $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$\nfor every $k$-CNF on $n \\ge 20$ declared variables, and\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$ when $m \\le Dn$ for a\nfixed $D > 0$; both with $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space (line 60). The\nsentence that follows the theorem is the one the title of this paper is\nabout (p.~2, lines 63--67):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker\nexponent $O_k(\\rho(n))$, where\n$\\rho(n) = n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n} = n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}$.\nAppendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.''\n\\end{quote}\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476) is the weaker bound as a\nstatement of its own: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$, the interpolation\nalgorithm counts in $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ bit operations and\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space, for $n \\ge 20$.\n\n\\paragraph{Which of the three is in the Lean.}\nThe comparator's \\runtimeScale{} (lines 132--134 of the comparator file)\nis $n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the squared form. The solution's\nown base-2 exponent \\texttt{rho}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is\n$n(\\log_2\\log_2 n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log_2 n}$, Proposition~6.4's $\\rho$ with the\nbase made explicit, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_le\\_four\\_rho} (lines\n190--191) bounds the comparator's exponent by four times it for\n$n \\ge 20$. The solution's sparsification parameters are Proposition~6.4's\nto the letter: the proof selects $\\lambda_n = \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$,\n$\\nu_n = \\lceil\\log_2\\lambda_n\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\lfloor\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}/\\nu_n^2\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\nu_n^4 \\le \\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486),\nand \\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define \\texttt{sparsifyLambda},\n\\texttt{sparsifyNu} and \\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three\nquantities in natural-number arithmetic. So the verified exponent is the\nweaker one, and the verified proof is organised around Section~6.4's\nparameters; what Appendix~D proves is not in the library in any form we\ncould find (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}). The general exponent\n$n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ and the fixed-density exponent\n$n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ are \\textbf{paper-only}: proved at p.~57 (lines\n3222--3246) and p.~54 (lines 3045--3064) respectively, by the contraction\ntheorem of Appendix~D.\n\n\\paragraph{Both bounds from one algorithm.}\nThe manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both paper-only\nbounds, switching on $m \\le n\\nu$ (p.~57, lines 3247--3250). That\nalgorithm is not the verified one either.\n\n\\paragraph{The three exponents, in numbers.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} puts the three exponents side by side,\neach divided by $n$ and each at constant $1$. The verified ratio is the\none of Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. The general paper-only ratio,\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$, never reaches $1$: it peaks at $2/e = 0.736$\nwhere $\\ln n = e^2$, that is $n = e^{e^2} \\approx 1{,}618$, and falls on\nboth sides. The fixed-density ratio $1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ decreases from\n$0.578$ at $n = 20$. So, at constant $1$, both paper-only guarantees sit\nbelow $2^n$ at every size, and the verified one sits above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ to $n \\approx 10^{2390}$. That is the whole content of the\ntitle's qualifier ``verified'': the manuscript's exponents would make a\ndifferent sentence, and they are not what the kernel checked. Two\ncautions apply to the table equally. The constants in front of every\nexponent are $O_k(1)$ or $O_{k,D}(1)$ and are not computed anywhere, in\nthe manuscript or in the Lean; and the manuscript's exponents are\nundefined below $n = e$ and are stated for $n \\ge 20$, while the\nverified one is stated for all $n$ (the $+16$). Figure~\\ref{fig:central}\ndraws the same three curves.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The three exponents of the manuscript divided by $n$, at constant\n$1$: the verified $\\rhon/n = (\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$\n(comparator lines 132--134); Theorem~1.1's general exponent\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 59); its fixed-density exponent\n$1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 62). Natural logarithms throughout (p.~1,\nlines 48--49). The row $e^{e^2}$ is the peak of the general ratio\n($2/e$). Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-exponents}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/beyond_exponents}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{The abstract's form.}\nThe abstract states the running time as\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}}$ (p.~1, lines 6--9). Since\n$(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = (\\ln n)^{o(1)}$, the verified exponent is of that form\ntoo, and the manuscript says so at lines 64--66. We tag the abstract's\nform \\textbf{verified}, with the remark that its $o(1)$ absorbs exactly\nthe factor the three rows of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} differ by:\nat $n = 10^6$ it is $(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = 6.9$ for the verified exponent,\n$\\ln\\ln n = 2.6$ for the general one and $1$ for the fixed-density one.\n\n\\subsection{The corollaries}\n\\label{sec:beyond-corollaries}\n\n\\paragraph{Finding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p.~2, lines 68--71).}\n``When $\\#F > 0$, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying\nassignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the\nremaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\nconditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying\nassignment,'' with polynomial expected overhead and a citation.\n\\textbf{Supported}: the self-reduction calls the verified counter at\nmost $n$ times on formulas of the same width, and the verified statement\nprovides the exact count it needs. The reduction is two sentences of\nprose and is not in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.2, Max-$k$-SAT within $\\varepsilon m$ (p.~2, lines 77--94).}\nA randomised algorithm finds an assignment satisfying at least\n$\\mathrm{OPT}(F) - \\varepsilon m$ clauses with probability\n$1 - 2^{-\\Omega(n)}$ in time\n\\[\n  (n+m)^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(\\rho(n))}\n\\]\n(line 80). The corollary is stated with $\\rho(n)$ itself, the\nverified exponent. Its proof samples $O(n/\\varepsilon^2)$ clauses,\nbuilds a threshold circuit of size $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$, reduces it to\na 3-CNF with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables by a cited lemma, and calls\nTheorem~1.1 with binary search and self-reduction (lines 82--94).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the exponent matches the verified one because\n$\\rho(cn) = O_c(\\rho(n))$, and every call is to a fixed-width counter\non $O(n)$ variables; the sampling, the circuit and the reduction are on\npaper. Its randomness is in the reduction, not in the counter.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.3, independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings (p.~2--3, lines 100--120).}\nThe number of independent sets and, for fixed $q$, of proper\n$q$-colourings of a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges in $2^{o(v)}$\ntime, and the number of perfect matchings in $2^{o(v+e)}$ time, all in\npolynomial space. The encodings are explicit: one variable per vertex\nand a 2-clause per edge (lines 107--112); $q$ variables per vertex with\nexactly-one and no-equal-colour clauses, a $q$-CNF on $qv$ variables\n(lines 112--115); one variable per edge and a linear-size circuit for\n``exactly one selected edge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with\n$O(v+e)$ variables, whose gate variables are determined by the edge\nbits so that the count is preserved (lines 116--119).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the conclusion is $2^{o(\\cdot)}$, which needs only\n$\\rhon = o(n)$ \\leanref{rho-little-o}, and each encoding is\ncounting-preserving into a fixed-width CNF on $O(v)$ or $O(v+e)$\nvariables. None of the three encodings is in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.4, a circuit lower bound for $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ (p.~3, lines 121--127).}\n``The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for\n$\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$'' (line 121): a language $L \\in \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\nand a constant $c > 0$ with $C_L(n) > c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ for infinitely\nmany $n$, where $C_L(n)$ counts non-input fan-in-two gates (lines\n123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967),\nWilliams's witness-circuit argument with a local reduction, and it\ninvokes the paper-only exponent in so many words: ``Put\n$a(t) = \\sqrt{\\log_2 t}$. Testing whether the count in the\nconstant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is positive decides 3-SAT with $V$\nvariables and at most $5V$ clauses in time $V^b\\,2^{AV/a(V)}$ for fixed\n$A, b > 0$'' (p.~52, lines 2949--2950); the threshold\n$s_n = \\lfloor\\eta n\\lfloor a(n)\\rfloor\\rfloor$ is chosen against that\n$a$ (line 2953), and the constant $c$ comes out as\n$\\sqrt{\\eta/(d\\log 2)}$ (line 2967). \\textbf{Paper-only}: the\nquantitative bound $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ is tied to $2^{AV/\\sqrt{\\log V}}$,\nwhich the library does not contain. What the same argument would give\nfrom the verified exponent is a question about the manuscript's proof,\nwhich we do not examine (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-not}).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}} \\not\\subseteq \\mathrm{SIZE}(O(n))$ (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}\n``Williams proved that if ETH is false, then $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\ndoes not have linear-size circuits [Wil13, Theorem 6.1]. Apply this\nimplication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\nsupplied by Theorem 1.1.'' \\textbf{Supported}: the only input is a\ndeterministic $2^{o(n)}$ 3-SAT decision, which the verified statement\nprovides on its answer tape for $k = 3$; the implication is a citation.\nThe manuscript's own scope sentence applies: the corollary ``does not\nassert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP'' (lines 2923--2924).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.2, inverting constant-locality maps (p.~52, lines 2974--2977).}\nFor a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits,\nthe preimage count $|G^{-1}(\\vec b)|$ and a preimage in deterministic\ntime $(n+m)^{O_d(1)}\\,2^{O_d(\\rho(n))}$ and polynomial space, for\n$n \\ge 20$. Stated with $\\rho(n)$. The proof writes one clause per\nforbidden local assignment, a CNF of width at most $d$ on the original\n$n$ variables with at most $2^d m$ clauses whose satisfying assignments\nare exactly the preimages, and applies Theorem~1.1 with\n$k = \\max\\{2, d\\}$ (lines 2979--2988). \\textbf{Supported}: the\nencoding is on paper; the counter and the exponent are the verified\nones. The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way\nfunctions (p.~53, lines 2994--2999), inherits the same status and the\nmanuscript's own limit, ``This does not rule out one-wayness against\npolynomial-time algorithms'' (line 2999).\n\n\\subsection{Appendix D: the contraction route}\n\\label{sec:beyond-appendix-d}\n\nAppendix~D, ``A sharper bound for exact counting'' (p.~53--64, from line\n3001), proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The\ntwo running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines\n3002--3003). Theorem~D.1 (p.~53, lines 3017--3028) is the contraction\ntheorem: a bipartite tensor network with at most $Cs$ edges, maximum\ndegree at most $\\lceil s^{9/10}\\rceil$ and entries in\n$(\\mathbb{Z}/2^{P_0}\\mathbb{Z})[V]/(V^\\ell)$ is contracted in\n$(s+L)^{O(1)}\\,2^{O(s/\\sqrt{\\log s})}$ bit operations and polynomial\nspace. The fixed-density proof (p.~54, lines 3045--3064) encodes a\nformula as a network with $O_k(n+m)$ edges and applies Theorem~D.1 with\n$s = n + m$; the arbitrary-density proof (p.~57, lines 3222--3246)\ncombines the sparsification of Section~6 with Lemma~D.2 (p.~54, line\n3069) and reaches the exponent $O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})$ at line\n3243. Section~D.4 changes the logarithm convention for its own proof,\n``log denotes a base-two logarithm and ln denotes a natural logarithm''\n(p.~57, lines 3251--3252). All of this is \\textbf{paper-only}: no\ndeclaration about tensor networks, contraction, Hermite recovery or\npackets exists in the family's 3,977 files, by the identifier search of\nSection~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}. We make no statement about Appendix~D's\nargument; we did not read it for correctness.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses of \\S1.1.1 and the fine-grained remarks of \\S1.1.3}\n\\label{sec:beyond-hypotheses}\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.1 (p.~4, lines 187--191).}\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [\\ldots] and SETH [\\ldots], and of Gap-ETH.''\nAnd \\S1.1.2 (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves\n\\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time and\ntherefore refutes \\#ETH.'' \\textbf{Supported}, and by the shortest\nargument in the manuscript: the verified statement is a deterministic\nmachine whose answer tape carries $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ and whose\ncount tape carries the count, within $2^{o(n)}$ times a polynomial, for\neach fixed $k \\ge 2$; each hypothesis is the negation of that. The\nhypotheses themselves are not defined in the library (the strings\n``ETH'' and ``SETH'' occur in the collection's Lean only in two files of\na different family, \\clonefile{lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/},\nwhich take an ETH-like proposition as a hypothesis and are not in the\nclosure). Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} states each hypothesis with its\nquantifiers and says exactly how the verified statement negates it; the\ntwo sentences the manuscript adds at lines 191--194, on unconditional\nlower bounds for restricted proof systems and on Williams's 2016\nMA-SETH protocol, are context, not claims about the result.\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.3 (p.~5, lines 213--243).}\nAfter a list of lower bounds proved from ETH or SETH (lines 213--225),\nthe manuscript writes: ``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis\nfor the hardness claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from\nweaker hypotheses that may still hold'' (lines 226--227), names the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture (lines 229--232) and the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$\nalgorithm, adding: ``Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each\nfixed clause width $k$, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow\nwith $n$'' (lines 235--236). These are \\textbf{paper-only} remarks in\nthe sense that nothing about any other hypothesis or any reduction is in\nthe library; they are also the manuscript's own statement of what the\nresult does not do, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} rests on them.\nThe one positive transfer, Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$\nby one variable per element and two clauses per set (lines 237--243), is\n\\textbf{supported} in the same sense as Corollary~1.3: an encoding on\npaper into the verified counter, inheriting $\\rhon$. The caveat at\np.~2, lines 95--99, that $2^{o(n)}$ bounds ``do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions,'' is not a claim but a limit the\nmanuscript places on its own result; it is quoted again in\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences}.\n\n\\subsection{What the library carries beyond the comparator statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond-lean}\n\n``Paper-only'' is defined against the whole library at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}, not only against the comparator file, so we say what\nelse the library proves about this family. \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}\n(201 lines) states five theorems, each followed by a \\texttt{\\#print\naxioms} directive (lines 197--201), and the replay's build log carries\nthe kernel's answer for all five: the three standard axioms\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms\\_in\\_build.txt}).\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 10) and\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 74): the comparator statement,\n  first over the explicit 80-register, 1,220-tape program and then over\n  the machine it compiles to (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} (lines 104--147): the same conclusion\n  with the polynomial factor written as $(n + |F| + 1)^{d}$ instead of\n  $(L+1)^d$, the exponent as $A\\cdot\\mathtt{rho}\\ n$ in base 2, and the\n  proviso $20 \\le n$ (line 109); its proof takes $2d$, $2e$ and $4A$ from\n  the comparator theorem's constants (line 132). This is Proposition~6.4's\n  $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ form in the library, so the\n  manuscript's polynomial factor is verified as well as its exponent; it\n  is \\textbf{verified}, and it is still the weaker exponent.\n\\item \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} (lines 149--183), for $k \\ge 3$:\n  the answer bit satisfies \\texttt{answer = true $\\leftrightarrow$\n  SAT.Satisfiable F}, the decision form made explicit.\n\\item \\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main} (line 184): the proposition\n  \\texttt{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean}, line 37), a decision-only\n  target for every width $k \\ge 3$ with the same \\texttt{runtimeBound},\n  stated there as a \\texttt{Prop} ``no inhabitant is supplied here'' and\n  inhabited in \\path{Final.lean}.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\begin{sloppypar}\nBeyond these, an identifier search over the family's 3,977 \\texttt{.lean}\nfiles, by the read-only script \\repofile{paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py},\nfinds no occurrence of \\texttt{SimpleGraph}, \\texttt{PerfectMatching},\n\\texttt{Coloring}, \\texttt{IndependentSet}, \\texttt{CircuitSize},\n\\texttt{OrthogonalVectors}, \\texttt{MaxSat}, \\texttt{TensorNetwork},\n\\texttt{HermiteInterp}, \\texttt{ENP}, \\texttt{SIZE},\n\\texttt{SetSplitting}, \\texttt{GapETH} or \\texttt{NSETH} as an\nidentifier: none of the corollaries, none of Appendix~D, and none of\nthe hypotheses is formalised. The comparator's JSON,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}, names one\ntheorem, \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, and no definition. A grep is\nnecessary and not sufficient (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); here it is used\nfor absence, where a false negative would require a corollary to be\nformalised under none of the words its own statement uses.\n\\end{sloppypar}\n\n\\subsection{The table}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{Every claim of the manuscript examined in this section: where it\nis stated (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of the committed extract),\nits status in the sense of Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-statuses}, and the\none-line reason. Generated, with every page and fragment checked against\nthe extract and the clone, by \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-claims}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/beyond_claims}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:beyond-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the paper-only proofs.} Appendix~D,\n  Appendix~C.1 and the two sharper bounds of Theorem~1.1 were located\n  and quoted, not read for correctness. ``Paper-only'' means not in the\n  Lean library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; it does not mean wrong, and\n  nothing here suggests it.\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the supported arguments either.} The\n  reductions behind Corollaries~1.2, 1.3, C.1, C.2 and the Set Splitting\n  transfer are standard in kind and short, and we checked only which\n  property of the counter they use. Whether each is correct as written\n  is the manuscript's, not ours.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the verified exponent would yield\n  through Corollary~1.4's argument.} A reader can rerun Appendix~C.1\n  with $a(t)$ replaced by the verified rate; we did not, because it is\n  a question about a paper-only proof.\n\\item \\textbf{No constants.} Every $O_k(1)$ and $O_{k,D}(1)$ in this\n  section is as unknown as the comparator's $C, A, S, d, e$; the\n  comparisons of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} are at constant $1$\n  and say nothing about any machine's running time.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the collection.} Whether the sharper bounds\n  have since been formalised elsewhere, or the manuscript revised, is\n  not examined; what is read is the commit and the dated manuscript.\n\\end{itemize}\n======\n% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05).\n% Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone\n% (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote\n% gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n% Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).\n\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n\\label{sec:statement}\n\nThe whole of what is machine-checked about family 378 is one theorem,\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, stated in the 220-line comparator file\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} of the\ncollection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nat commit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The file carries the statement with a\n\\texttt{sorry} for its proof (line 218); the solution tree proves the same\nstatement, and the collection's comparator checks that the two agree\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). The replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\nrebuilt that proof and found the three standard axioms and nothing else.\nThis section reads the statement, definition by definition, and says what\nit asserts and what it does not. Line numbers are those of the comparator\nfile; we quote nothing from memory.\n\n\\subsection{The definitions, one by one}\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a\n\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a\n\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function\n\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean\n``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some\nliteral is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''\n(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most\n\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,\n\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments\n(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the\nnumber of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when\n\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the\nmeaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is\nallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\nallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\nmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\nliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\nends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\nelement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\nA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\nat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\nnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\nlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\nTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\nso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\nis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\nso\n\\[\n  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n\\]\nfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\ndefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\na formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\nan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\neach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\ndomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\nanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01T7tyqb5CDjvS16quMVedJL","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
