{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KvmBD4bvfLirAHJb1S6U5o","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t                  THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE\n2\t\n3\t                                                      OPENAI\n4\t\n5\t\n6\t        Abstract. We give a deterministic algorithm for exactly counting the satisfying assignments of\n7\t        an arbitrary k-CNF formula. For every fixed k ⩾ 2, on a formula with n variables and m clauses,\n8\t                                                                                         1 +o(1)\n9\t        our algorithm uses polyk (n, m) space and runs in time (n + m)Ok (1) 2 n/(log n) 2 . Our algorithm\n10\t        refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH), which postulates that deciding 3-SAT on n variables\n11\t        requires 2Ω(n) time in the worst case.\n12\t\n13\t\n14\t\n15\t\n16\t                                                    Contents\n17\t  1. Introduction                                                                                             1\n18\t  2. Technique overview                                                                                       7\n19\t  3. A fast counting algorithm via shared linear summaries                                                   21\n20\t  4. Constructing low-degree replacement forms                                                               26\n21\t  5. Counting common zeros of sparse polynomial equations                                                    35\n22\t  6. Reducing #k-SAT to counting common zeros                                                                39\n23\t  Appendix A. Partitioning the satisfying assignments                                                        44\n24\t  Appendix B. Exact arithmetic and space                                                                     47\n25\t  Appendix C. Additional Consequences of Refuting ETH                                                        51\n26\t  Appendix D. A sharper bound for exact counting                                                             53\n27\t  References                                                                                                 64\n28\t\n29\t\n30\t\n31\t                                               1. Introduction\n32\t   For every fixed k ⩾ 3, k-SAT is the canonical NP-complete problem [Coo71], and its exponential-\n33\ttime complexity underlies the Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) and the Strong Exponential\n34\tTime Hypothesis (SETH) [IP01, IPZ01]. In this paper, we refute ETH by giving, for every fixed\n35\tk ⩾ 2, a deterministic algorithm that counts the satisfying assignments of a k-CNF in subexponential\n36\ttime and polynomial space.\n37\t   Write the input k-CNF on n declared variables with m input clauses as\n38\t                                                m\n39\t                                                ^ \u0002                                         \u0003\n40\t                         F (x1 , . . . , xn ) =    (xie,1 ⊕ be,1 ) ∨ · · · ∨ (xie,k ⊕ be,k ) ,\n41\t                                              e=1\n42\t\n43\twhere ie,j ∈ {1, . . . , n} and be,j ∈ {0, 1} are the variable index and negation bit of the jth literal in\n44\tclause e, and ⊕ denotes addition modulo two. Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than k\n45\tliterals. The indices ie,j and bits be,j are fixed by F ; only x1 , . . . , xn vary over assignments. Our aim\n46\tis to compute the quantity\n47\t                                      #F = {x ∈ {0, 1}n : F (x) = 1} .                                   (1.1)\n48\tTesting #F > 0 also decides satisfiability. We keep k fixed as n grows. Throughout, log denotes the\n49\tnatural logarithm.\n50\t\n51\t  Date: 17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026.\n52\t                                                          1\n53\t\f2                                                  OPENAI\n54\t\n55\t\n56\tTheorem 1.1. For