{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LM9rE75GBawdJpzNtiCScY","type":"tool_result","content":"300:of the sparsification lemma [IPZ01], with quantitative bounds from [CIP06, Section 6] and [Cal09,\n995:with quantitative bounds from [CIP06, Section 6] and [Cal09, Lemma 5.14 and equation (5.4)].\n2523:accounting of [CIP06, Section 6]. The refinement that makes the two cases disjoint is standard in\n3682:[Cal09]   Chris Calabro. The Exponential Complexity of Satisfiability Problems. Ph.D. dissertation, University of\n3684:[CIP06]   Chris Calabro, Russell Impagliazzo, and Ramamohan Paturi. A Duality between Clause Width and Clause\n=== refs to consequences ===\npaper3/latex/sec3_beyond.tex:296:closure). Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} states each hypothesis with its\npaper3/latex/sec3_beyond.tex:314:result does not do, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} rests on them.\npaper3/latex/sec3_beyond.tex:322:Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}.\n=== helpers ===\ndef read_lines(path):\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return f.read().split('\\n')\n\n\ndef page_of(lines, lineno):\n    \"\"\"Page of paper.pdf = form feeds strictly before the line + 1 (form feeds on\n    the line itself open the next page, so a line that starts with one is on it).\"\"\"\n    ff = sum(l.count('\\f') for l in lines[:lineno - 1])\n    ff += lines[lineno - 1].count('\\f')\n    return ff + 1\n\n\ndef check_fragments(lines, a, b, fragments, label):\n    block = '\\n'.join(lines[a - 1:b])\n    ok = True\n    for frag in fragments:\n        if frag not in block:\n            print(f'  FAIL {label}: fragment not found in lines {a}-{b}: {frag!r}')\n            ok = False\n    return ok\n\n\ndef main():\n    check_only = '--check' in sys.argv\n    failures = 0\n\n    # 1. The manuscript.\n    paper = read_lines(PAPER_TXT)\n=== table writer ===\n329:            f.write('\\\\begin{tabularx}{\\\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\\\raggedright\\\\arraybackslash}p{0.33\\\\linewidth}'\n330-                    '>{\\\\raggedright\\\\arraybackslash}p{0.10\\\\linewidth}'\n331-                    '>{\\\\raggedright\\\\arraybackslash}p{0.10\\\\linewidth}X@{}}\\n')\n332-            f.write('\\\\toprule\\nclaim & stated & status & why \\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\n')\n333-            for claim, where, status, why in rows:\n334-                f.write(f'{claim} & {where} & {status} & {why} \\\\\\\\\\n')\n335:            f.write('\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabularx}\\n')\n336-        with open(os.path.join(TABLES, 'beyond_exponents.tex'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n337-            f.write('% generated by python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py; do not edit\\n')\n338:            f.write('\\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}\\n\\\\toprule\\n')\n339-            f.write('$n$ & verified $\\\\rhon/n$ & general $\\\\ln\\\\ln n/\\\\sqrt{\\\\ln n}$ & density $1/\\\\sqrt{\\\\ln n}$ \\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\n')\n340-            for label, v, g, d in exp_rows:\n341-                f.write(f'${label}$ & {v:.3f} & {g:.3f} & {d:.3f} \\\\\\\\\\n')\n342:            f.write('\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\n')\n343-        print(f'\\nwrote {TABLES}/beyond_claims.tex and beyond_exponents.tex')\n344-\n345-    print(f'\\n{\"ALL CHECKS PASSED\" if failures == 0 else str(failures) + \" CHECK(S) FAILED\"}')\n346-    return 0 if failures == 0 else 1\n347-\n348-\n349-if __name__ == '__main__':\n350-    sys.exit(main())\n=== sec7 stub ===\n% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 06 drafts this section: a SAT solver and a model counter on random 3-CNF at $n = 20\\ldots 60$ against $2^{\\rhon}$ and $2^n$ (figure labelled \\texttt{fig:practitioner}, from \\repofile{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}); the verified guarantee is never the binding one on any machine. Ends with what this does not claim''.}\n=== plan 0.5 item 05 ===\n1-# Paper 3 — *Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.* — plan for Ralph loop0015\n2-\n3:*Written 2026-11-05, after the two audits of OpenAI's family 378\n4-(`reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md`, `proof_audit.md`) and\n5-after loop0014's items 01–02 fixed what the verified statement says and\n6-restated it in Lean. The title is fixed by the owner and is used verbatim,\n7-including the ellipsis and the final period, everywhere. The loop\n8-directory is `Ralph_Loops/loop0015/` (`TASK.md`, `iterations.md`,\n9-`PROGRESS.md`, `loop.py`, `gate.py`, `knobs.json`, `allowed_sorries.txt`,\n10-`budget_watch.py`), cloned from loop0014's. Not launched.*\n11-\n12-**This paper is about the limitation of the ETH refutation and nothing\n13:else.** (Owner, 2026-11-05.) It is not concerned with graphs, pathwidth,\n14-treewidth, MOSP or any width parameter.\n15-\n16-## 0. The question and the stance\n17-\n18-### 0.1 The question\n19-\n20-On 2026-10-25 OpenAI's manuscript collection (`github.com/openai/math`,\n21-Apache-2.0; cloned read-only under `~/dev/untrusted/openai-math` at commit\n22-`4eaee875`) added family 378, *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*:\n23-a deterministic algorithm that counts the satisfying assignments of any\n24-`k`-CNF on `n` variables in time subexponential in `n`, with a Lean proof\n25-checked by the collection's comparator mechanism against a 220-line\n26-statement. The paper asks one question and answers it by reading, not by\n27-opinion:\n28-\n29-> **Q.** What exactly is verified, what is only in the manuscript, what\n30-> follows from the verified part, and what does it change for anyone who\n31-> solves or counts CNF formulas on a machine?\n32-\n33-### 0.2 The stance, and the title's claim made precise\n34-\n35:The two audits of 2026-11-05 found **no flaw**: the comparator statement\n36-means what the manuscript's verified form says, and the proof's import\n37-closure shows no unsoundness by inspection. The paper is therefore **a\n38-careful reading by people who checked, not a debunking**, and every\n39-sentence is to be written so that a reader who admires the result finds\n40-nothing unfair in it. What the title asserts, made precise:\n41-\n42-1. **What is verified is a bound, a guarantee**: for each `k ≥ 2` there\n43-   *exist* a finite multitape machine and constants `C, A, S > 0`,\n44-   `d, e ≥ 1` such that on every width-`≤ k` CNF the machine halts with\n45-   the exact count within `C (L + 1)^d · 2^{A · ρ(n)}` steps, where `L`\n46-   is the encoding's bit length and\n47-   `ρ(n) = n (ln ln(n + 16))² / √ln(n + 16)`. The constants are\n48-   existential: the proof constructs the machine from an explicit program\n49-   but **no value of `C, A, S, d, e` or of the state count is computed**\n50-   anywhere in the library.\n51-2. **The exponent is `ρ(n)`, and only `ρ(n)`.** The manuscript's Theorem\n52-   1.1 states the sharper `n log log n / √log n` and, at fixed clause\n53-   density, `n / √log n`; the Lean library carries neither\n54-   (`lean/docs/378.md` says so). Those exponents are **unverified**.\n55-3. **The factor `ρ(n)/n` exceeds 1 for every `50 ≤ n < e^{5504}`**: it\n--\n72-### 0.3 What is on record (nothing to redo)\n73-\n74:- `reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md` (2026-11-05): the\n75-  comparator statement read field by field — `PrimitiveMachine` is\n76-  finite, `Trace` counts exactly the steps, the encoding is `O(k·n·m)`\n77-  bits and unary in `n`, the quantifier order puts the machine and the\n78-  five constants after `k` and before `n` and `F`, `runtimeScale` is\n79-  `ρ(n)` in natural logarithms, the output is the count in binary on a\n80-  named tape and the decision in the halting state; verdict \"not\n81-  refuted\", with the `ρ(n)/n` numbers and the companion-claims list\n82-  (what the statement supports, what is paper-only). Scripts beside it:\n83-  `check_encoding.py`, `VarCheck.lean`.\n84:- `reports/scratch/eth_audit/proof_audit