{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgj89Vw5mwFTuYKkBRZ67","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NG8S1ZcCQ99RRY75bUvDwm","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex","content":"% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n% and written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name.\n\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified statement,\ndrew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the proof and\nreplayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement\nalone: which hypotheses it refutes and by what step, and what a lower\nbound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The\nmanuscript answers both questions itself, in \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5)\nand \\S1.1.3 (p.~5), and we quote it; what we add is the quantifier form\nof each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that negates\nit, and the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name, and\nnothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same conjuncts of\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (comparator file lines 194--217,\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}): for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (line 38), halts\nwith \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines 203--204, 147),\nthe bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and in its halting\nstate (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps (lines\n210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} \\texttt{step} is a function and\n  \\texttt{Trace} is its iteration (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there\n  is no random tape and no nondeterministic choice. A deterministic\n  algorithm is also a bounded-error randomised algorithm, with error\n  $0$, and also a nondeterministic algorithm, with no guess; so each\n  refutation of a deterministic hypothesis is a refutation of its\n  randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is $2^{o(n)}$ in $n$ alone.} A \\texttt{Finset} of\n  clauses of width at most $k$ over $n$ variables has at most\n  $\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j}$ members ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n  $k = 3$), so $m = O(n^k)$ and $L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); hence the bound is\n  $n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every $\\delta > 0$ there is an\n  $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which it is below $2^{\\delta n}$. Below\n  $n_0$ the bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the\n  running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$,\n  and $2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out. This\n  is an asymptotic fact and it is the only kind of fact the hypotheses\n  are about; it coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation\n  that $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where\n  the asymptotics begin.\n\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$ and $F$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ proviso in the\n  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform\n  negation: a single algorithm that is $O(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta$ at once, where a hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$\n  there is an algorithm'' would have allowed a different algorithm for\n  each $\\delta$.\n\\item \\textbf{The model is the conservative one.} The comparator's\n  machine is a multitape Turing machine with one head per tape, moving\n  one cell or writing one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine\n  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n  input is the manuscript's canonical encoding, of length polynomial in\n  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n  too.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n\nThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n\\end{quote}\nand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\nsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\nand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\nmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nand say which conjunct negates it. Table~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the\nsummary. In every case the only thing used is the property of\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used from the\nmanuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\nasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\nworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\nTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In quantifiers:\nthere is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps on all inputs.\nIts negation is: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one. The verified\nstatement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the same machine,\nwhose answer bit is $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$, that is, the decision,\nwithin $O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}, item 2). This holds on every\ninput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\nof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\nlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\ngives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\nHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\nwith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\nproblems (lines 169--171). The negation is: there is an\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed $k$ there is a\ndeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm. The verified\nstatement gives more: for \\emph{every} $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every\n$k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs within\n$C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and $A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$\nfor all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$ because $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$\nthe exponential factor is a constant. So each $k$ has a\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ algorithm, and the polynomial's\ndegree depending on $k$ is exactly what the hypothesis's ``for a fixed\n$k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass through ETH; it reads the\nstatement at each $k$. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH: if\n3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the Sparsification Lemma of\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces $k$-SAT on $n$ variables\nto $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and\nso, after the clause-splitting reduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$\nvariables, so would $k$-SAT for every fixed $k$; so the refutation of\nETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the implication for the\nrecord; the direct reading above is what the paper rests on.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\nfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\nsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\nciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\nthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\nat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\nciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. The negation is: for every\n$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\nseparates the two cases, already at $m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates\nthem, since a formula in which every assignment violates a positive\nfraction of the clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at\n$k = 3$ decides within $n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$\nsteps for every $\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (item 2 above; at $m = O(n)$\nthe polynomial is $O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine\nanswers correctly on every input, promised or not, with probability $1$,\nwhich refutes the randomised form by item 1.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. The negation is:\nthere is an $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a\nnondeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for\nunsatisfiability. The verified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false}\nexactly when $\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides\nunsatisfiability deterministically within the SETH bound of the previous\nparagraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and every $k$; and a deterministic\nmachine is a nondeterministic one whose only computation path is the\ndeterministic run, so every acceptance convention for nondeterministic\ntime is met. The manuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and\nunsatisfiability'' (line 188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. The negation is: for\nevery $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm\nthat outputs the exact count. The verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with\n\\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape, a binary word of length\nat most $n + 1$ that decodes to $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204,\n208--209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which\nthe decision bit is not enough and the count tape is what refutes it;\nit is also the hypothesis the comparator statement was written for, the\ncount being the theorem's first conclusion and the decision its\ncorollary.\n\n\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\nThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\nmay be randomised and must be correct with probability bounded away\nfrom $1/2$ on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an\nalgorithm with error $0$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH\nabove refute these forms by the same machine, with no change to the\nbound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we state the\nforms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact\nerror threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\ncited section and definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\nthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\nthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nthe quantifier form, and the conjunct of the comparator statement that\nnegates it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the existential constants of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\nchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\nTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\nthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\nbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\nand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\nin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower\nbound proved unconditionally for a restricted proof system is not a\nstatement about algorithms in general and is not touched; the second\nthat one member of the family was already false before this result.\nNeither is used below.\n\n\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems in polynomial time, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe state what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of\nthe two standard shapes,\n\\[\n  \\text{(E)}\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\n  \\qquad\n  \\text{(S)}\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n\\]\nwhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever parameter of $X$ and $Y$ the theorem\nnames and $c$ is its constant. Nothing below depends on what $X$ and $Y$\nare.\n\n\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\nA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an implication, proved by a reduction\nfrom $k$-SAT to $X$ or $Y$ that turns a fast algorithm for the target\ninto a fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The reduction is unaffected by\nanything in the verified statement; the implication remains a theorem.\nWhat has changed is that its premise is now known to be false\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the implication can no\nlonger be discharged: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\nwhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n\n\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\nA false premise does not make a conclusion false. The statement ``no\n$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\ncomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\nelse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\nthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\nopposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\nexactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\na target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\nother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\nnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\np.~2, lines 95--99:\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\nthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\nsubexponential time algorithms.''\n\\end{quote}\nSo for shape (E): a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\nreduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\nis $O(n)$ in $X$'s parameter, and the manuscript gives one such transfer\n(below). For shape (S): a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$ would\nneed the exponent of the SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a\nconstant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is\n$A\\rhon'$ in the variable count $n'$ of the SAT instance with $A$ unknown\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); no value of $A$ is on record below $257$,\nand no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any $Y$. No\nstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\nanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\nA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\na reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated parameter\ncontrol. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still true as\na statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a fast\nalgorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer\nconveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\nrefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\nlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem with\na false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either way,\nand nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors. The\nsecond is the width-unrestricted form of SETH, which we quote with the\nmanuscript's list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\nvisible in its quantifiers (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the machine\nand the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and nothing in\nthe statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula whose width\ngrows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single bound in the\nstatement at all; even the trivial observation that every $k$-CNF is a\nCNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis quantifies over all widths\nat once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound\nthat follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n\n\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\nThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\nsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per universe\nelement and two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per\nuniverse element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential\ntime in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,''\nciting \\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}\ntags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the\nverified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee\non the SAT instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the\nnumber of SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it:\nat $A = 1$ the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\nunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a $2^{o(n')}$\nalgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\nthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\nnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\nread through a reduction.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a\n  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n  value, which is unknown.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n  discussed here, and no example from any other parameterisation is\n  given, by the scope of this paper.\n\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n  reader should check the source.\n\\item \\textbf{Asymptotic only.} Every refutation here is the negation of\n  an asymptotic statement and uses only $\\rhon = o(n)$. None says that\n  any machine decides or counts anything fast at any size; the constants\n  are as unknown here as in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the\n  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\\end{itemize}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7404,"cache_read_input_tokens":165081,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7404,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"670fc671-9721-48eb-82d9-ea6bc5bd38c4","uuid":"2e25425b-3b10-467c-9e31-98c1ded5609e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:10:27.444Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj89VWHwcXsK5FnhLsdr","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01NG8S1ZcCQ99RRY75bUvDwm":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex","content":"% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n% and written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name.\n\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified statement,\ndrew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the proof and\nreplayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement\nalone: which hypotheses it refutes and by what step, and what a lower\nbound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The\nmanuscript answers both questions itself, in \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5)\nand \\S1.1.3 (p.~5), and we quote it; what we add is the quantifier form\nof each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that negates\nit, and the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name, and\nnothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same conjuncts of\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (comparator file lines 194--217,\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}): for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (line 38), halts\nwith \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines 203--204, 147),\nthe bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and in its halting\nstate (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps (lines\n210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} \\texttt{step} is a function and\n  \\texttt{Trace} is its iteration (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there\n  is no random tape and no nondeterministic choice. A deterministic\n  algorithm is also a bounded-error randomised algorithm, with error\n  $0$, and also a nondeterministic algorithm, with no guess; so each\n  refutation of a deterministic hypothesis is a refutation of its\n  randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is $2^{o(n)}$ in $n$ alone.} A \\texttt{Finset} of\n  clauses of width at most $k$ over $n$ variables has at most\n  $\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j}$ members ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n  $k = 3$), so $m = O(n^k)$ and $L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); hence the bound is\n  $n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every $\\delta > 0$ there is an\n  $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which it is below $2^{\\delta n}$. Below\n  $n_0$ the bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the\n  running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$,\n  and $2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out. This\n  is an asymptotic fact and it is the only kind of fact the hypotheses\n  are about; it coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation\n  that $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where\n  the asymptotics begin.\n\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$ and $F$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ proviso in the\n  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform\n  negation: a single algorithm that is $O(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta$ at once, where a hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$\n  there is an algorithm'' would have allowed a different algorithm for\n  each $\\delta$.\n\\item \\textbf{The model is the conservative one.} The comparator's\n  machine is a multitape Turing machine with one head per tape, moving\n  one cell or writing one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine\n  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n  input is the manuscript's canonical encoding, of length polynomial in\n  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n  too.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n\nThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n\\end{quote}\nand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\nsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\nand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\nmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nand say which conjunct negates it. Table~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the\nsummary. In every case the only thing used is the property of\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used from the\nmanuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\nasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\nworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\nTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In quantifiers:\nthere is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps on all inputs.\nIts negation is: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one. The verified\nstatement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the same machine,\nwhose answer bit is $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$, that is, the decision,\nwithin $O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}, item 2). This holds on every\ninput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\nof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\nlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\ngives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\nHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\nwith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\nproblems (lines 169--171). The negation is: there is an\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed $k$ there is a\ndeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm. The verified\nstatement gives more: for \\emph{every} $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every\n$k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs within\n$C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and $A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$\nfor all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$ because $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$\nthe exponential factor is a constant. So each $k$ has a\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ algorithm, and the polynomial's\ndegree depending on $k$ is exactly what the hypothesis's ``for a fixed\n$k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass through ETH; it reads the\nstatement at each $k$. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH: if\n3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the Sparsification Lemma of\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces $k$-SAT on $n$ variables\nto $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and\nso, after the clause-splitting reduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$\nvariables, so would $k$-SAT for every fixed $k$; so the refutation of\nETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the implication for the\nrecord; the direct reading above is what the paper rests on.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\nfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\nsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\nciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\nthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\nat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\nciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. The negation is: for every\n$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\nseparates the two cases, already at $m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates\nthem, since a formula in which every assignment violates a positive\nfraction of the clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at\n$k = 3$ decides within $n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$\nsteps for every $\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (item 2 above; at $m = O(n)$\nthe polynomial is $O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine\nanswers correctly on every input, promised or not, with probability $1$,\nwhich refutes the randomised form by item 1.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. The negation is:\nthere is an $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a\nnondeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for\nunsatisfiability. The verified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false}\nexactly when $\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides\nunsatisfiability deterministically within the SETH bound of the previous\nparagraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and every $k$; and a deterministic\nmachine is a nondeterministic one whose only computation path is the\ndeterministic run, so every acceptance convention for nondeterministic\ntime is met. The manuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and\nunsatisfiability'' (line 188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. The negation is: for\nevery $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm\nthat outputs the exact count. The verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with\n\\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape, a binary word of length\nat most $n + 1$ that decodes to $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204,\n208--209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which\nthe decision bit is not enough and the count tape is what refutes it;\nit is also the hypothesis the comparator statement was written for, the\ncount being the theorem's first conclusion and the decision its\ncorollary.\n\n\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\nThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\nmay be randomised and must be correct with probability bounded away\nfrom $1/2$ on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an\nalgorithm with error $0$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH\nabove refute these forms by the same machine, with no change to the\nbound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we state the\nforms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact\nerror threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\ncited section and definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\nthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\nthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nthe quantifier form, and the conjunct of the comparator statement that\nnegates it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the existential constants of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\nchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\nTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\nthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\nbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\nand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\nin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower\nbound proved unconditionally for a restricted proof system is not a\nstatement about algorithms in general and is not touched; the second\nthat one member of the family was already false before this result.\nNeither is used below.\n\n\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems in polynomial time, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe state what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of\nthe two standard shapes,\n\\[\n  \\text{(E)}\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\n  \\qquad\n  \\text{(S)}\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n\\]\nwhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever parameter of $X$ and $Y$ the theorem\nnames and $c$ is its constant. Nothing below depends on what $X$ and $Y$\nare.\n\n\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\nA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an implication, proved by a reduction\nfrom $k$-SAT to $X$ or $Y$ that turns a fast algorithm for the target\ninto a fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The reduction is unaffected by\nanything in the verified statement; the implication remains a theorem.\nWhat has changed is that its premise is now known to be false\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the implication can no\nlonger be discharged: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\nwhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n\n\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\nA false premise does not make a conclusion false. The statement ``no\n$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\ncomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\nelse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\nthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\nopposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\nexactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\na target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\nother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\nnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\np.~2, lines 95--99:\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\nthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\nsubexponential time algorithms.''\n\\end{quote}\nSo for shape (E): a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\nreduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\nis $O(n)$ in $X$'s parameter, and the manuscript gives one such transfer\n(below). For shape (S): a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$ would\nneed the exponent of the SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a\nconstant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is\n$A\\rhon'$ in the variable count $n'$ of the SAT instance with $A$ unknown\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); no value of $A$ is on record below $257$,\nand no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any $Y$. No\nstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\nanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\nA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\na reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated parameter\ncontrol. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still true as\na statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a fast\nalgorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer\nconveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\nrefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\nlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem with\na false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either way,\nand nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors. The\nsecond is the width-unrestricted form of SETH, which we quote with the\nmanuscript's list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\nvisible in its quantifiers (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the machine\nand the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and nothing in\nthe statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula whose width\ngrows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single bound in the\nstatement at all; even the trivial observation that every $k$-CNF is a\nCNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis quantifies over all widths\nat once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound\nthat follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n\n\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\nThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\nsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per universe\nelement and two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per\nuniverse element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential\ntime in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,''\nciting \\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}\ntags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the\nverified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee\non the SAT instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the\nnumber of SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it:\nat $A = 1$ the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\nunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a $2^{o(n')}$\nalgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\nthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\nnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\nread through a reduction.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a\n  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n  value, which is unknown.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n  discussed here, and no example from any other parameterisation is\n  given, by the scope of this paper.\n\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n  reader should check the source.\n\\item \\textbf{Asymptotic only.} Every refutation here is the negation of\n  an asymptotic statement and uses only $\\rhon = o(n)$. None says that\n  any machine decides or counts anything fast at any size; the constants\n  are as unknown here as in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the\n  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\\end{itemize}\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NG8S1ZcCQ99RRY75bUvDwm","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"670fc671-9721-48eb-82d9-ea6bc5bd38c4","uuid":"f4e3da07-f291-4909-aa3f-0ab73c02f830","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:10:27.466Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex","content":"% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n% and written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name.\n\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified statement,\ndrew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the proof and\nreplayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement\nalone: which hypotheses it refutes and by what step, and what a lower\nbound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The\nmanuscript answers both questions itself, in \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5)\nand \\S1.1.3 (p.~5), and we quote it; what we add is the quantifier form\nof each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that negates\nit, and the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name, and\nnothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same conjuncts of\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (comparator file lines 194--217,\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}): for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (line 38), halts\nwith \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines 203--204, 147),\nthe bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and in its halting\nstate (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps (lines\n210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} \\texttt{step} is a function and\n  \\texttt{Trace} is its iteration (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there\n  is no random tape and no nondeterministic choice. A deterministic\n  algorithm is also a bounded-error randomised algorithm, with error\n  $0$, and also a nondeterministic algorithm, with no guess; so each\n  refutation of a deterministic hypothesis is a refutation of its\n  randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is $2^{o(n)}$ in $n$ alone.} A \\texttt{Finset} of\n  clauses of width at most $k$ over $n$ variables has at most\n  $\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j}$ members ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n  $k = 3$), so $m = O(n^k)$ and $L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); hence the bound is\n  $n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every $\\delta > 0$ there is an\n  $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which it is below $2^{\\delta n}$. Below\n  $n_0$ the bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the\n  running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$,\n  and $2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out. This\n  is an asymptotic fact and it is the only kind of fact the hypotheses\n  are about; it coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation\n  that $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where\n  the asymptotics begin.\n\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$ and $F$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ proviso in the\n  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform\n  negation: a single algorithm that is $O(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta$ at once, where a hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$\n  there is an algorithm'' would have allowed a different algorithm for\n  each $\\delta$.\n\\item \\textbf{The model is the conservative one.} The comparator's\n  machine is a multitape Turing machine with one head per tape, moving\n  one cell or writing one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine\n  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the\n  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n  input is the manuscript's canonical encoding, of length polynomial in\n  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n  too.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n\nThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n\\end{quote}\nand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\nsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\nand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\nmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nand say which conjunct negates it. Table~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the\nsummary. In every case the only thing used is the property of\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used from the\nmanuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\nasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\nworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\nTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In quantifiers:\nthere is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps on all inputs.\nIts negation is: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one. The verified\nstatement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the same machine,\nwhose answer bit is $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$, that is, the decision,\nwithin $O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}, item 2). This holds on every\ninput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\nof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\nlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\ngives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\nHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\nwith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\nproblems (lines 169--171). The negation is: there is an\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed $k$ there is a\ndeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm. The verified\nstatement gives more: for \\emph{every} $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every\n$k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs within\n$C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and $A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$\nfor all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$ because $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$\nthe exponential factor is a constant. So each $k$ has a\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ algorithm, and the polynomial's\ndegree depending on $k$ is exactly what the hypothesis's ``for a fixed\n$k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass through ETH; it reads the\nstatement at each $k$. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH: if\n3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the Sparsification Lemma of\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces $k$-SAT on $n$ variables\nto $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and\nso, after the clause-splitting reduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$\nvariables, so would $k$-SAT for every fixed $k$; so the refutation of\nETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the implication for the\nrecord; the direct reading above is what the paper rests on.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\nfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\nsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\nciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\nthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\nat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\nciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. The negation is: for every\n$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\nseparates the two cases, already at $m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates\nthem, since a formula in which every assignment violates a positive\nfraction of the clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at\n$k = 3$ decides within $n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$\nsteps for every $\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (item 2 above; at $m = O(n)$\nthe polynomial is $O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine\nanswers correctly on every input, promised or not, with probability $1$,\nwhich refutes the randomised form by item 1.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. The negation is:\nthere is an $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a\nnondeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for\nunsatisfiability. The verified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false}\nexactly when $\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides\nunsatisfiability deterministically within the SETH bound of the previous\nparagraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and every $k$; and a deterministic\nmachine is a nondeterministic one whose only computation path is the\ndeterministic run, so every acceptance convention for nondeterministic\ntime is met. The manuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and\nunsatisfiability'' (line 188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. The negation is: for\nevery $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm\nthat outputs the exact count. The verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with\n\\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape, a binary word of length\nat most $n + 1$ that decodes to $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204,\n208--209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which\nthe decision bit is not enough and the count tape is what refutes it;\nit is also the hypothesis the comparator statement was written for, the\ncount being the theorem's first conclusion and the decision its\ncorollary.\n\n\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\nThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\nmay be randomised and must be correct with probability bounded away\nfrom $1/2$ on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an\nalgorithm with error $0$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH\nabove refute these forms by the same machine, with no change to the\nbound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we state the\nforms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact\nerror threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\ncited section and definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\nthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\nthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nthe quantifier form, and the conjunct of the comparator statement that\nnegates it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the existential constants of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\nchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\nTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\nthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\nbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\nand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\nin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower\nbound proved unconditionally for a restricted proof system is not a\nstatement about algorithms in general and is not touched; the second\nthat one member of the family was already false before this result.\nNeither is used below.\n\n\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems in polynomial time, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe state what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of\nthe two standard shapes,\n\\[\n  \\text{(E)}\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\n  \\qquad\n  \\text{(S)}\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n\\]\nwhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever parameter of $X$ and $Y$ the theorem\nnames and $c$ is its constant. Nothing below depends on what $X$ and $Y$\nare.\n\n\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\nA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an implication, proved by a reduction\nfrom $k$-SAT to $X$ or $Y$ that turns a fast algorithm for the target\ninto a fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The reduction is unaffected by\nanything in the verified statement; the implication remains a theorem.\nWhat has changed is that its premise is now known to be false\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the implication can no\nlonger be discharged: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\nwhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n\n\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\nA false premise does not make a conclusion false. The statement ``no\n$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\ncomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\nelse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\nthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\nopposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\nexactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\na target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\nother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\nnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\np.~2, lines 95--99:\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\nthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\nsubexponential time algorithms.''\n\\end{quote}\nSo for shape (E): a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\nreduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\nis $O(n)$ in $X$'s parameter, and the manuscript gives one such transfer\n(below). For shape (S): a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$ would\nneed the exponent of the SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a\nconstant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is\n$A\\rhon'$ in the variable count $n'$ of the SAT instance with $A$ unknown\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); no value of $A$ is on record below $257$,\nand no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any $Y$. No\nstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\nanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\nA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\na reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated parameter\ncontrol. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still true as\na statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a fast\nalgorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer\nconveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\nrefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\nlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem with\na false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either way,\nand nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors. The\nsecond is the width-unrestricted form of SETH, which we quote with the\nmanuscript's list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\nvisible in its quantifiers (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the machine\nand the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and nothing in\nthe statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula whose width\ngrows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single bound in the\nstatement at all; even the trivial observation that every $k$-CNF is a\nCNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis quantifies over all widths\nat once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound\nthat follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n\n\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\nThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\nsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per universe\nelement and two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per\nuniverse element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential\ntime in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,''\nciting \\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}\ntags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the\nverified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee\non the SAT instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the\nnumber of SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it:\nat $A = 1$ the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\nunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a $2^{o(n')}$\nalgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\nthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\nnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\nread through a reduction.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a\n  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n  value, which is unknown.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n  discussed here, and no example from any other parameterisation is\n  given, by the scope of this paper.\n\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n  reader should check the source.\n\\item \\textbf{Asymptotic only.} Every refutation here is the negation of\n  an asymptotic statement and uses only $\\rhon = o(n)$. None says that\n  any machine decides or counts anything fast at any size; the constants\n  are as unknown here as in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the\n  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\\end{itemize}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":5,"newStart":1,"newLines":404,"lines":["-% What follows -- drafted by loop0015 item 05.","-\\section{What follows}","+% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05).","+% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of","+% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);","+% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n","+% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked","+% and written by","+%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py","+% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms","+% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no","+% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name.","+\\section{What follows, in general terms}"," \\label{sec:consequences}"," ","-\\placeholder{Item 05 drafts this section: which hypotheses the verified statement refutes (ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, \\#ETH, NSETH, the randomised forms) and how; in general terms, that a lower bound derived from ETH or SETH loses its premise while a statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is not itself refuted (the manuscript's \\S1.1.3); no example from any width parameter. Ends with what this does not claim''.}","+Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified statement,","+drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the proof and","+replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement","+alone: which hypotheses it refutes and by what step, and what a lower","+bound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The","+manuscript answers both questions itself, in \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5)","+and \\S1.1.3 (p.~5), and we quote it; what we add is the quantifier form","+of each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that negates","+it, and the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose","+premise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing","+here names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name, and","+nothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two","+sections state.","+","+\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}","+\\label{sec:consequences-property}","+","+Every refutation below rests on the same conjuncts of","+\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (comparator file lines 194--217,","+Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one","+deterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any","+\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (line 38), halts","+with \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines 203--204, 147),","+the bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and in its halting","+state (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps (lines","+210--211). Four features of that property do the work.","+","+\\begin{enumerate}","+\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} \\texttt{step} is a function and","+  \\texttt{Trace} is its iteration (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there","+  is no random tape and no nondeterministic choice. A deterministic","+  algorithm is also a bounded-error randomised algorithm, with error","+  $0$, and also a nondeterministic algorithm, with no guess; so each","+  refutation of a deterministic hypothesis is a refutation of its","+  randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same machine.","+\\item \\textbf{The bound is $2^{o(n)}$ in $n$ alone.} A \\texttt{Finset} of","+  clauses of width at most $k$ over $n$ variables has at most","+  $\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j}$ members ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,","+  $k = 3$), so $m = O(n^k)$ and $L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})$","+  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); hence the bound is","+  $n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$","+  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every $\\delta > 0$ there is an","+  $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which it is below $2^{\\delta n}$. Below","+  $n_0$ the bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the","+  running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$,","+  and $2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out. This","+  is an asymptotic fact and it is the only kind of fact the hypotheses","+  are about; it coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation","+  that $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where","+  the asymptotics begin.","+\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the","+  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$ and $F$","+  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ proviso in the","+  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one","+  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong, uniform","+  negation: a single algorithm that is $O(2^{\\delta n})$ for every","+  $\\delta$ at once, where a hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$","+  there is an algorithm'' would have allowed a different algorithm for","+  each $\\delta$.","+\\item \\textbf{The model is the conservative one.} The comparator's","+  machine is a multitape Turing machine with one head per tape, moving","+  one cell or writing one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine","+  is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models in which the","+  hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at","+  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The","+  input is the manuscript's canonical encoding, of length polynomial in","+  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial","+  too.","+\\end{enumerate}","+","+\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}","+\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}","+","+The manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):","+\\begin{quote}\\small","+``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing","+whether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability","+and unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for","+every fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise","+problem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error","+randomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,","+Definition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''","+\\end{quote}","+and, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)","+solves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time","+and therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the","+manuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,","+and say which conjunct negates it. Table~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the","+summary. In every case the only thing used is the property of","+Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used from the","+manuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified","+(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).","+","+\\paragraph{ETH.}","+As stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)","+asserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the","+worst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,","+Theorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In quantifiers:","+there is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides","+3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps on all inputs.","+Its negation is: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one. The verified","+statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the same machine,","+whose answer bit is $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$, that is, the decision,","+within $O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps","+(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}, item 2). This holds on every","+input of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading","+of ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all","+large $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where","+$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an","+$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement","+gives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.","+","+\\paragraph{SETH.}","+As stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time","+Hypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires","+$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every","+$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic","+algorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''","+with the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open","+problems (lines 169--171). The negation is: there is an","+$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed $k$ there is a","+deterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm. The verified","+statement gives more: for \\emph{every} $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every","+$k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs within","+$C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and $A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$","+for all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$ because $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$","+the exponential factor is a constant. So each $k$ has a","+$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ algorithm, and the polynomial's","+degree depending on $k$ is exactly what the hypothesis's ``for a fixed","+$k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass through ETH; it reads the","+statement at each $k$. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH: if","+3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the Sparsification Lemma of","+\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces $k$-SAT on $n$ variables","+to $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and","+so, after the clause-splitting reduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$","+variables, so would $k$-SAT for every fixed $k$; so the refutation of","+ETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the implication for the","+record; the direct reading above is what the paper rests on.","+","+\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}","+As stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis","+(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$","+for which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a","+satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a","+$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''","+citing \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and","+(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with","+the same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability","+at least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''","+citing \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. The negation is: for every","+$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that","+separates the two cases, already at $m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates","+them, since a formula in which every assignment violates a positive","+fraction of the clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at","+$k = 3$ decides within $n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$","+steps for every $\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (item 2 above; at $m = O(n)$","+the polynomial is $O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine","+answers correctly on every input, promised or not, with probability $1$,","+which refutes the randomised form by item 1.","+","+\\paragraph{NSETH.}","+As stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong","+Exponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every","+$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic","+$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a","+$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a","+tautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. The negation is:","+there is an $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a","+nondeterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for","+unsatisfiability. The verified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false}","+exactly when $\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides","+unsatisfiability deterministically within the SETH bound of the previous","+paragraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and every $k$; and a deterministic","+machine is a nondeterministic one whose only computation path is the","+deterministic run, so every acceptance convention for nondeterministic","+time is met. The manuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and","+unsatisfiability'' (line 188) is this.","+","+\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}","+As stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time","+Hypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying","+assignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time","+[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. The negation is: for","+every $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm","+that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with","+\\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape, a binary word of length","+at most $n + 1$ that decodes to $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204,","+208--209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which","+the decision bit is not enough and the count tape is what refutes it;","+it is also the hypothesis the comparator statement was written for, the","+count being the theorem's first conclusion and the decision its","+corollary.","+","+\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}","+The manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):","+``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,","+Definition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm","+may be randomised and must be correct with probability bounded away","+from $1/2$ on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an","+algorithm with error $0$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH","+above refute these forms by the same machine, with no change to the","+bound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we state the","+forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact","+error threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the","+cited section and definition.","+","+\\begin{table}[htbp]","+\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in","+the form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of","+the committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,","+the quantifier form, and the conjunct of the comparator statement that","+negates it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).","+$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the existential constants of","+Section~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment","+checked against the extract and the clone, by","+\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}","+\\label{tab:hypotheses}","+\\scriptsize","+\\input{tables/hypotheses}","+\\end{table}","+","+\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}","+Two sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):","+the result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower","+bounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''","+and ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH","+(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability","+in $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''","+\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower","+bound proved unconditionally for a restricted proof system is not a","+statement about algorithms in general and is not touched; the second","+that one member of the family was already false before this result.","+Neither is used below.","+","+\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}","+\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}","+","+The manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were","+proved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with","+exponential algorithms and for problems in polynomial time, and then","+says what its result does to them (lines 226--227):","+\\begin{quote}\\small","+``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness","+claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker","+hypotheses that may still hold.''","+\\end{quote}","+We state what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of","+the two standard shapes,","+\\[","+  \\text{(E)}\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,","+  \\qquad","+  \\text{(S)}\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,","+\\]","+where $n$ and $k$ are whatever parameter of $X$ and $Y$ the theorem","+names and $c$ is its constant. Nothing below depends on what $X$ and $Y$","+are.","+","+\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}","+A theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an implication, proved by a reduction","+from $k$-SAT to $X$ or $Y$ that turns a fast algorithm for the target","+into a fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The reduction is unaffected by","+anything in the verified statement; the implication remains a theorem.","+What has changed is that its premise is now known to be false","+(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the implication can no","+longer be discharged: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the","+whole of what ``removes one basis'' means.","+","+\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}","+A false premise does not make a conclusion false. The statement ``no","+$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no","+$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the","+comparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing","+else. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and","+the verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the","+opposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says","+exactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for","+a target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the","+other direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the","+number of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at","+p.~2, lines 95--99:","+\\begin{quote}\\small","+``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under","+general polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for","+this), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit","+subexponential time algorithms.''","+\\end{quote}","+So for shape (E): a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$ follows only from a","+reduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables","+is $O(n)$ in $X$'s parameter, and the manuscript gives one such transfer","+(below). For shape (S): a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$ would","+need the exponent of the SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a","+constant factor, and the verified statement's exponent is","+$A\\rhon'$ in the variable count $n'$ of the SAT instance with $A$ unknown","+(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); no value of $A$ is on record below $257$,","+and no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any $Y$. No","+statement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by","+anything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.","+","+\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}","+A statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that","+a reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated parameter","+control. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still true as","+a statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a fast","+algorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer","+conveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is","+refuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no","+longer a fact about $Y$'s running time.","+","+\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}","+The manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the","+Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:","+``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies","+OVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem with","+a false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either way,","+and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors. The","+second is the width-unrestricted form of SETH, which we quote with the","+manuscript's list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233--236):","+\\begin{quote}\\small","+``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that","+unrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time","+algorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem","+1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,","+while this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''","+\\end{quote}","+The verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is","+visible in its quantifiers (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the machine","+and the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and nothing in","+the statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula whose width","+grows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single bound in the","+statement at all; even the trivial observation that every $k$-CNF is a","+CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis quantifies over all widths","+at once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound","+that follows from it, is exactly where it was.","+","+\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}","+The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of","+size at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per universe","+element and two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per","+universe element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential","+time in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,''","+citing \\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}","+tags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the","+verified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee","+on the SAT instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the","+number of SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it:","+at $A = 1$ the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every","+$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$","+unknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified","+statement through a variable-controlling reduction is a $2^{o(n')}$","+algorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,","+than trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is","+not a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,","+read through a reduction.","+","+\\subsection{What this does not claim}","+\\label{sec:consequences-not}","+","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is","+  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a","+  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth","+  value, which is unknown.","+\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is","+  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that","+  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such","+  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.","+\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is","+  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved","+  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says","+  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond","+  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The","+  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not","+  discussed here, and no example from any other parameterisation is","+  given, by the scope of this paper.","+\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated","+  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;","+  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in","+  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,","+  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the","+  reader should check the source.","+\\item \\textbf{Asymptotic only.} Every refutation here is the negation of","+  an asymptotic statement and uses only $\\rhon = o(n)$. None says that","+  any machine decides or counts anything fast at any size; the constants","+  are as unknown here as in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the","+  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's","+  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the","+  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence","+  of what is verified and is not claimed here.","+\\end{itemize}"]}],"originalFile":"% What follows -- drafted by loop0015 item 05.\n\\section{What follows}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 05 drafts this section: which hypotheses the verified statement refutes (ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, \\#ETH, NSETH, the randomised forms) and how; in general terms, that a lower bound derived from ETH or SETH loses its premise while a statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is not itself refuted (the manuscript's \\S1.1.3); no example from any width parameter. Ends with what this does not claim''.}\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NG8S1ZcCQ99RRY75bUvDwm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
